{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>The scepter shall not depart from Judah.</b> From David onward — these are the heads of the diaspora in Babylonia, who rule the people with the staff appointed by authority of the kingdom (Bereshit Rabbah)." "<b>And the lawgiver from between his feet.</b> The disciples — these are the princes of the land of Israel." "<b>Until Shiloh come.</b> King Messiah, whose is the kingdom. And so Onkelos rendered it. Its midrash: שִׁילוֹ — שַׁי לוֹ, as it says, ״יֹבִילוּ שַׁי לַמּוֹרָא״ (Psalms 76)." "<b>And to him shall the obedience of the peoples be.</b> The gathering of the peoples — for the yod is essential in the root, as in ״יִפְעָתֶךָ״ (Ezekiel 28), and sometimes it falls away from it. And several letters serve in this manner, and they are called the fallen radical — such as the nun of נוֹגֵף and of נוֹשֵׁךְ, and the alef of ״אַוְחֲוָתִי בְּאָזְנֵיכֶם״ (Job 13) and of ״אִבְחַת חֶרֶב״ (Ezekiel 21), and ״אָסוּךְ שָׁמֶן״ (II Kings 4). So too here: יִקְהַת עַמִּים — the gathering of peoples, as it says, ״אֵלָיו גּוֹיִם יִדְרֹשׁוּ״ (Isaiah 11). And similar to it: ״עַיִן תִּלְעַג לְאָב וְתָבוּז לִיקְהַת אֵם״ (Proverbs 30) — for the gathering of wrinkles in her face on account of old age; and in the Talmud: דְּיָתְבֵי וּמַקְהוּ אַקְהָתָא בְּשׁוּקֵי דִּנְהַרְדְּעָא, in tractate Yevamot. And it could have said קְהִיַּת עַמִּים."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:04:09.089509463Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}